WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.625
This video explains BMS, the building
management system, and how its network

2
00:00:07.625 --> 00:00:10.245
connections fit into your work.

3
00:00:11.595 --> 00:00:18.040
We will cover what a building management
system is, what the guide says about it, how

4
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.134
its information travels, what you do, and what
to ask.

5
00:00:26.284 --> 00:00:30.060
BMS stands for Building Management System.

6
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:38.625
It is a control system that monitors and
manages a building's mechanical and electrical

7
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:39.343
systems.

8
00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:48.263
In general, that means things like power and
cooling equipment, water leak detection, fire

9
00:00:48.263 --> 00:00:50.571
safety, lighting and access.

10
00:00:51.921 --> 00:00:59.411
Sensors and controllers send their readings
over a network to the BMS, which shows them on

11
00:00:59.411 --> 00:01:05.319
screens and raises an alarm when something
goes outside its safe range.

12
00:01:06.669 --> 00:01:10.658
So a BMS can only see what its network can
reach.

13
00:01:11.408 --> 00:01:13.904
That is where your cabling comes in.

14
00:01:15.254 --> 00:01:17.387
That was general background.

15
00:01:18.137 --> 00:01:25.732
The guide itself only describes the BMS
network connections in the end of row racks.

16
00:01:26.482 --> 00:01:31.154
It does not list which building systems are
monitored in this hall.

17
00:01:35.304 --> 00:01:41.640
There are two types of end of row rack: the
standard one, and the one with BMS.

18
00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:49.134
There are six BMS racks: 611 and 613 in pod
ten.

19
00:01:50.484 --> 00:02:00.447
Then 1411 and 1413 in pod eleven, and 2211 and
2213 in pod twelve.

20
00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:04.695
Each pair is in the middle SPOCS row of its
pod.

21
00:02:06.045 --> 00:02:13.896
A BMS rack adds two things: an FB0 BMS switch,
and a copper panel for it.

22
00:02:16.196 --> 00:02:24.285
Its management trunks are twenty-four fiber,
and duplex three on each one carries the FB0

23
00:02:24.285 --> 00:02:24.921
uplink.

24
00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:31.111
The guide also lists blue copper as BMS, if
your site stocks it.

25
00:02:35.261 --> 00:02:38.824
Let us trace BMS information through the hall.

26
00:02:39.824 --> 00:02:48.400
It starts at BMS devices, which connect by
copper to the FB0 switch and its copper panel.

27
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:55.123
The guide does not say which devices these
are, or who runs those cables.

28
00:02:56.473 --> 00:03:04.969
The FB0 switch uplinks with a two-fiber LC
cord into the front of the RU thirty-nine LC

29
00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:08.484
panel, at the duplex three position.

30
00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:17.001
Behind that position, a twenty-four fiber
trunk carries it to management spine one, and

31
00:03:17.001 --> 00:03:20.543
another carries it to management spine two.

32
00:03:21.893 --> 00:03:23.067
Why two?

33
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:31.065
Every end of row rack connects to both
management spines, so the management network,

34
00:03:31.065 --> 00:03:33.481
BMS included, has two paths.

35
00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:41.017
Past the spines, the information reaches the
systems that collect and display it.

36
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.922
That part is not yours.

37
00:03:48.072 --> 00:03:51.272
So what do you actually do at a BMS rack?

38
00:03:52.272 --> 00:03:53.744
Identify it.

39
00:03:54.494 --> 00:04:02.736
It is marked EOR BMS on the map, and you
should find the FB0 switch and an extra copper

40
00:04:02.736 --> 00:04:03.305
panel.

41
00:04:04.655 --> 00:04:05.785
Connect it.

42
00:04:06.535 --> 00:04:14.112
Land the twenty-four fiber trunks on the rear
of the RU thirty-nine panel, and plug the

43
00:04:14.112 --> 00:04:20.295
switch cords into the front, including duplex
three for the FB0 switch.

44
00:04:21.645 --> 00:04:27.469
Label it: wrap-around labels on the trunks,
and flag labels on the cords.

45
00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:30.270
And verify it.

46
00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.601
Both trunks are twenty-four fiber.

47
00:04:34.351 --> 00:04:40.474
The FB0 cord is in duplex three for both
spines, per the cutsheet.

48
00:04:41.224 --> 00:04:43.293
The SPOCS cords are in.

49
00:04:44.043 --> 00:04:46.881
And the far ends are patched before turn-up.

50
00:04:51.031 --> 00:04:55.319
Some BMS details are simply not in the guide.

51
00:04:56.069 --> 00:04:58.565
Bring these questions to your lead.

52
00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:04.727
Which rack unit the FB0 switch and its copper
panel use.

53
00:05:06.077 --> 00:05:13.106
Which BMS devices connect to it, where their
cables come from, and whether your crew runs

54
00:05:13.106 --> 00:05:13.501
them.

55
00:05:14.851 --> 00:05:21.678
What FB0 stands for, and whether any testing
is required on BMS links.

56
00:05:23.028 --> 00:05:24.308
Do not guess.

57
00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:31.714
A missing BMS connection may not show up until
the day someone needs an alarm.

58
00:05:35.864 --> 00:05:43.850
In lesson fourteen of the course, trace the
BMS uplink step by step and answer a question

59
00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.016
at each stop.

